---
Part V, Chapter 14: The failure catalog
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The failure catalog

A run of Loom can fail. The disk may refuse a loom, the store may not install a language's package, and the workspace's settings may name anchor delimiters that Loom cannot use. Effect carries such a failure in the type of the step that may fail. Each module of Loom keeps its own errors that way. An error is a class with a tag, which names the failure, and fields, which hold what the module knows about it.

A failure is not a fault. A fault is a fact about a loom's text, such as an anchor that names no section. It has a place in the loom, and it stands for as long as the text does. The chapter "The fault catalog" names every fault. A failure is an event of one run. It has no place in a loom's text, and it ends with the run. Mixed line endings are a fact about the text, so they are a fault. A loom that the disk refuses is an event, so it is a failure.

The two catalogs keep one rule. The step that detects a failure names it, says what it knows, and never writes the words a person reads. This module is the one place that gives a failure its words. Its table holds a case for every failure of the catalog, and the compiler checks that it does. A failure new to Loom does not compile until the table gives it words.

Loom tells a person about a failure in two programs, the command line and the language server. Each program tells a failure through a function that takes only a failure of the catalog. A failure the catalog does not name cannot reach that function, so a program that may raise one does not compile.

## The words of a failure

The type `Problem` holds what a person reads about one failure. Its `summary` is one sentence that says what went wrong. Its `detail` says why, or what to do, and may be empty. The function `problem` makes one.

A failure often holds a `cause`, which is whatever the system or a package threw. The function `reasonOf` reads a cause as a sentence a person may read. A cause may itself be a failure of this catalog, as a loom that was not read is when a step dies on it. Such a failure writes no message of its own, so the function `saidBy` takes the line this catalog has for it. Of any other error it takes the error's own message, or the error's name when the message is empty, and it takes any other value as text. A cause comes in another program's words and in that program's layout. It may run over several lines, as a parser's message does when it quotes the text it stopped at, and it may not end as a sentence. So the function `firstParagraph` keeps what stands before the first blank line, on one line, without a colon left hanging at its end. The function `closed` then gives it a full stop when it lacks one.

The type `Wording` shapes a table with one case for each failure of a union. A case takes the failure of its own tag and gives that failure's problem. The compiler demands a case for every tag the union has, and refuses a case for a tag the union does not have.

=>

export type Problem = { readonly summary: string; readonly detail: string }

export const problem = (summary: string, detail = ''): Problem => ({ summary, detail })

const closed = (words: string): string => (/(^|[.!?])$/.test(words) ? words : `${words}.`)

const firstParagraph = (text: string): string =>
  (text.split(/\n\s*\n/)[0] ?? '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().replace(/:$/, '')

const saidBy = (cause: unknown): string => {
  if (isWordedBy(failureWording)(cause)) return lineOf(describe(cause))
  return cause instanceof Error ? cause.message || cause.name : String(cause)
}

export const reasonOf = (cause: unknown): string => closed(firstParagraph(saidBy(cause)))

export type Wording<Failure extends { readonly _tag: string }> = {
  readonly [Tag in Failure['_tag']]: (failure: Extract<Failure, { readonly _tag: Tag }>) => Problem
}

## Reading a workspace

Loom reads a workspace before it tangles, weaves or checks it, and two things can stop the reading.

The disk may refuse a loom, a folder or a settings file, and so may the storage that keeps a remote workspace. A command may also name a path where nothing is. The corpus builder's error `ReadError` holds the `path` that was not read and the `cause`.

The workspace's settings may name anchor delimiters that Loom cannot use. The function `checkAnchorDelims` raises one of four errors, which the type `InvalidAnchorDelims` gathers. A delimiter is empty. One text opens and closes an anchor. A delimiter holds white space. Or the opening delimiter is a mark Loom already uses, such as the `#` of a heading. Each of the four ends on the same advice, which names the setting and the pair Loom uses when the settings name none.

=>

export type ReadingFailure = ReadError | InvalidAnchorDelims

const delimiterAdvice = `Set \`anchor\` in \`.loom/config.yaml\` to a pair that Loom does not use and your code never writes, such as \`${defaultAnchorDelims.open}\` and \`${defaultAnchorDelims.close}\`.`

const readingWording: Wording<ReadingFailure> = {
  ReadError: ({ path, cause }) => problem(`Loom could not read ${path}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  EmptyAnchorDelims: () => problem('An anchor delimiter cannot be empty.', delimiterAdvice),
  IdenticalAnchorDelims: ({ delim }) =>
    problem(`An anchor cannot open and close with the same \`${delim}\`.`, delimiterAdvice),
  WhitespaceAnchorDelims: () =>
    problem('An anchor delimiter cannot hold white space.', delimiterAdvice),
  ReservedAnchorDelims: ({ marker }) =>
    problem(`An anchor cannot open with \`${marker}\`, which Loom already uses.`, delimiterAdvice),
}

## Writing what Loom makes

Loom writes the files a tangle composes and the lock that records them. It writes a woven corpus, and a loom whose boxes it brought back in step. It removes a file that lost its loom. The disk may refuse any of these, as a folder Loom may not write to does. The tangler's error `NotWritten` holds the `path` Loom could not write and the `cause`. Its error `NotRemoved` holds the same for a file Loom could not remove. A command that changes the workspace's settings writes the settings file, and the configuration's error `SettingsNotWritten` holds the same for that file.

=>

export type WritingFailure = NotWritten | NotRemoved | SettingsNotWritten

const writingWording: Wording<WritingFailure> = {
  NotWritten: ({ path, cause }) => problem(`Loom could not write ${path}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  NotRemoved: ({ path, cause }) => problem(`Loom could not remove ${path}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  SettingsNotWritten: ({ path, cause }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not write ${path}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
}

## A tangle refused

Loom says what is wrong with a loom in the loom's diagnostics. A diagnostic holds a message, a place in the loom and a severity. A tangle writes no file while a loom it reaches holds a diagnostic that stops it, such as an error or a section still to be settled.

The compiler's error `TangleError` holds the `entry` the tangle started from and the `failures`, which are the diagnostics of each loom. The detail a person reads counts them, in the plain word for them: one problem, or 3 problems.

The function `refusedBy` gives one line for each of those diagnostics: its loom, its line in the loom, and its message, which the fault catalog wrote. The command line prints the lines under the failure. A test reads them to see what refused a tangle.

=>

export type TanglingFailure = TangleError

export const refusedBy = ({ failures }: TangleError): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  failures.flatMap(({ path, diagnostics }) =>
    diagnostics.map(({ position, message }) => `${path}:${position.start.line}: ${message}`),
  )

const howMany = (count: number): string => (count === 1 ? 'one problem' : `${count} problems`)

const tanglingWording: Wording<TanglingFailure> = {
  TangleError: (refused) =>
    problem(
      `Loom did not tangle ${refused.entry}.`,
      `Its looms hold ${howMany(refusedBy(refused).length)}.`,
    ),
}

## A package that serves a language

A language's service comes to a workspace as a package, which Loom installs into the workspace's store, as Part VII, Languages as packages, describes. Four things can fail around such a package.

- **The store cannot install a package.** Its error `StoreError` holds the `packageName` and the `cause`.
- **The language server cannot fetch its TypeScript.** The server downloads a TypeScript of its own. The error `TypeScriptNotFetched` holds the `version`, the `folder` the download was meant for, and the `cause`.
- **A project's TypeScript 6 is missing, or does not load.** The server's error `ClassicTypeScriptMissing` holds the `folder` Loom looked from. Its `tsdk` is the folder of a TypeScript that Loom found and could not load, and is none when Loom found no TypeScript.
- **A service fails at what it is asked,** such as formatting a section. The error `ServiceError` holds the `service` and the `reason`, in the service's own words.

=>

export type PackageFailure =
  StoreError | TypeScriptNotFetched | ClassicTypeScriptMissing | ServiceError

const packageWording: Wording<PackageFailure> = {
  StoreError: ({ packageName, cause }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not install ${packageName}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  TypeScriptNotFetched: ({ version, folder, cause }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not fetch TypeScript ${version} into ${folder}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  ClassicTypeScriptMissing: ({ folder, tsdk, cause }) =>
    Option.match(tsdk, {
      onNone: () =>
        problem(
          `Loom found no TypeScript 6 for ${folder}, in the project or beside Loom.`,
          'Install it in the project with `npm install -D typescript@6`.',
        ),
      onSome: (found) => problem(`The TypeScript in ${found} did not load.`, reasonOf(cause)),
    }),
  ServiceError: ({ service, reason }) =>
    problem(`The ${service} language service failed.`, reasonOf(reason)),
}

## Keeping relations

An author approves a reference, and Loom keeps the approval in the workspace, as the chapter "Fingerprints on relations" describes. The workspace may not take the write. The error `RelationsNotKept` holds the `workspace` and the `cause`. A workspace kept in remote storage never takes one, and the error `StorageTakesNoWrite` says so of its `workspace`.

=>

export type KeepingFailure = RelationsNotKept | StorageTakesNoWrite

const keepingWording: Wording<KeepingFailure> = {
  RelationsNotKept: ({ workspace, cause }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not keep the relations of ${workspace}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  StorageTakesNoWrite: ({ workspace }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not keep the relations of ${workspace}.`, 'Remote storage takes no write.'),
}

## An application asks too much

An application that shows a workspace's looms in words of its own asks the language server for an outline and for edits, as the chapters "The outline of a loom" and "Edits an application asks for" describe. The server cannot read every request it is sent. The error `RequestUnread` holds the `request` and the `cause`, the reason the request's parameters do not read.

A request may name a loom that is not there. The error `NoSuchLoom` holds its `path`. An edit may also ask for what cannot be done, and each refusal is an error of the edits: `LoomExists`, `NoSuchBook`, `BookExists`, `NoSuchPart`, `PartExists`, `PartNameIsNumber`, `TitleTaken`, `NotInPart`, `NotOfKind`, `NoSuchSection`, `SectionExists`, `OpensTheLoom`, `NoSuchNote`, `NotADiscussion`, `UnfitName`, `UnfitText`, `UnfitParameter`, `TakesNoParameters`, `OpensABook`, `NoConfiguration`, and `EditBreaksLoom`, which the chapter "Edits an application asks for" describes. A variable the edit would set under a name that is no name meets the configuration's error `NotAVariableName`, as `loom set` does. Each says what it refuses and names the fix, in words an application may show its writer or translate into its own. The function `bookWords` names a book in a sentence, and names the default book, which has no name, as the default book. A parameter that cannot stand in braces is refused for one of three reasons, and the table `unfitParameterWords` gives each its own words and its own fix.

=>

export type AskingFailure =
  | RequestUnread
  | NoSuchLoom
  | LoomExists
  | NoSuchBook
  | BookExists
  | NoSuchPart
  | PartExists
  | PartNameIsNumber
  | TitleTaken
  | NotInPart
  | NotOfKind
  | NoSuchSection
  | SectionExists
  | OpensTheLoom
  | NoSuchNote
  | NotADiscussion
  | UnfitName
  | UnfitText
  | UnfitParameter
  | TakesNoParameters
  | OpensABook
  | NoConfiguration
  | NotAVariableName
  | EditBreaksLoom

const bookWords = (book: string): string => (book === '' ? 'the default book' : `the book ${book}`)

const unfitParameterWords: { readonly [Why in UnfitParameter['why']]: (key: string) => Problem } = {
  word: (key) =>
    problem(
      `The parameter ${JSON.stringify(key)} cannot be a key in braces.`,
      'Name it with a letter or an underscore, followed by letters, digits, hyphens, and underscores.',
    ),
  field: (key) =>
    problem(
      `The parameter ${JSON.stringify(key)} has a field of its own.`,
      'Give the kind or the language in its own field, not among the parameters.',
    ),
  line: (key) =>
    problem(
      `A value of the parameter ${JSON.stringify(key)} runs over more than one line.`,
      'Keep each value on one line.',
    ),
}

const askingWording: Wording<AskingFailure> = {
  RequestUnread: ({ request, cause }) =>
    problem(`Loom could not read the parameters of ${request}.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  NoSuchLoom: ({ path }) =>
    problem(
      `The workspace holds no loom at ${path}.`,
      'Name a loom the workspace holds, or make it first.',
    ),
  LoomExists: ({ path }) =>
    problem(`A loom is already kept at ${path}.`, 'Choose another place for the new loom.'),
  NoSuchBook: ({ book }) =>
    problem(
      `No loom names the book ${book}.`,
      'Make the book first, or name a book the workspace holds.',
    ),
  BookExists: ({ book }) =>
    problem(`A loom already opens the book ${book}.`, 'Choose another name for the book.'),
  NoSuchPart: ({ book, part }) =>
    problem(
      `No part ${part} stands in ${bookWords(book)}.`,
      'Name a part by its number or its name, as the contents list it.',
    ),
  PartExists: ({ book, part }) =>
    problem(
      `A part named ${part} already stands in ${bookWords(book)}.`,
      'Choose another name for the part.',
    ),
  PartNameIsNumber: ({ name }) =>
    problem(
      `The name ${name} reads as a part's number.`,
      'Give the part a name that is no Roman numeral.',
    ),
  TitleTaken: ({ title, book, part }) =>
    problem(
      `A loom titled ${title} already stands in part ${part} of ${bookWords(book)}.`,
      'Choose another title, since two looms of one part cannot share one.',
    ),
  NotInPart: ({ path }) =>
    problem(
      `No loom of that part stands at ${path}.`,
      'Name a loom of the part to follow, or leave it out to place the loom last.',
    ),
  NotOfKind: ({ path, kind }) =>
    problem(
      `The loom ${path} is no ${kind}, and each kind of loom is numbered apart.`,
      `Name a ${kind} of the part to follow, or leave it out to place the loom last.`,
    ),
  NoSuchSection: ({ path, section }) =>
    problem(
      `The loom ${path} holds no section named ${section}.`,
      'Name a section by its heading, as the outline gives it.',
    ),
  SectionExists: ({ path, section }) =>
    problem(
      `The loom ${path} already holds a section named ${section}.`,
      'Choose another name, since two sections of one loom cannot share one.',
    ),
  OpensTheLoom: ({ path, section }) =>
    problem(
      `The section ${section} opens the loom ${path} and carries its title.`,
      'Remove or move the loom itself, or retitle it.',
    ),
  NoSuchNote: ({ path, section }) =>
    problem(
      `The section ${section} of ${path} holds no note with those words.`,
      'Name the note by its whole text, as the outline gives it.',
    ),
  NotADiscussion: ({ path }) =>
    problem(`The loom ${path} is no discussion.`, 'Open topics and add messages in a discussion.'),
  UnfitName: ({ name }) =>
    problem(
      `The name ${JSON.stringify(name)} cannot stand in a loom.`,
      'Give a name that holds words, on one line, with no brace or square bracket where it stands on a heading or a box.',
    ),
  UnfitText: ({ line }) =>
    problem(
      `The line ${JSON.stringify(line)} would read as Loom's syntax, not as words.`,
      'Reword it so it does not open as a heading, an arrow, a tilde, a divider, or a box does, and close any fence it opens.',
    ),
  UnfitParameter: ({ key, why }) => unfitParameterWords[why](key),
  TakesNoParameters: ({ path, section }) =>
    problem(
      `The section ${section} of ${path} opens with a division, whose line holds no braces.`,
      'Give parameters to a section that opens with a heading.',
    ),
  OpensABook: ({ path }) =>
    problem(
      `The loom ${path} opens a book, so it stands in no part.`,
      'Move the chapters of the book instead.',
    ),
  NoConfiguration: ({ workspace }) =>
    problem(
      `The workspace ${workspace} has no configuration.`,
      'Give it a configuration in `.loom/config.yaml` first.',
    ),
  NotAVariableName: ({ name }) =>
    problem(
      `The name \`${name}\` is not a variable name.`,
      'A name holds letters, digits and underscores, and does not open on a digit.',
    ),
  EditBreaksLoom: ({ path, problems }) =>
    problem(
      `The edit would leave ${path} with ${howMany(problems.length)} it does not hold now.`,
      closed(problems.join(' ')),
    ),
}

## A warning the run goes on after

Some failures do not end a run. A workspace whose settings file does not read is built with Loom's own defaults. A language whose package does not load goes unchecked, and the other languages load. An installed trope that Loom refuses adds no kinds, and the book is read without them. Each is still a failure of the run, and a person should hear of it.

The step that meets one logs the error itself as a warning, through Effect's logger, and goes on. It writes no words. Six errors are logged this way.

- **The settings file does not read.** The configuration's error `SettingsUnread` holds the `path` of the file and the `cause`.
- **A language's package is at another version.** The loader's error `PackageStale` holds the `language`, the `packageName`, the version the store `held` and the version this Loom `wanted`. The loader loads the package all the same.
- **A language's package is not in the store.** The error `PackageMissing` holds the `language` and the `packageName`.
- **A package exports no service for its language.** The error `PackageWithoutService` holds the same two.
- **A package does not load.** The error `PackageNotLoaded` holds them and the `cause`.
- **An installed trope is refused.** The error `TropeRefused` holds the `module`, which is the trope's loom, and the `reason` the registry of kinds gave.

A program's logger then words what was logged. The function `failureIn` reads a log's message and gives the failure it holds, when the message is one of a wording's failures. A program builds its logger on it, with its own wording, and prints the failure where it prints any other. A message that holds no failure is logged as before.

=>

export type Warning =
  | SettingsUnread
  | PackageStale
  | PackageMissing
  | PackageWithoutService
  | PackageNotLoaded
  | TropeRefused

const warningWording: Wording<Warning> = {
  SettingsUnread: ({ path, cause }) =>
    problem(
      `Loom could not read ${path}.`,
      `${reasonOf(cause)} Loom goes on with its own defaults.`,
    ),
  PackageStale: ({ language, packageName, held, wanted }) =>
    problem(
      `The store holds ${packageName} at ${held}, and this Loom is ${wanted}.`,
      `Run \`loom update ${language}\` to bring it along.`,
    ),
  PackageMissing: ({ language, packageName }) =>
    problem(
      `The language ${language} is activated, and ${packageName} is not installed.`,
      `Run \`loom update ${language}\` to install it.`,
    ),
  PackageWithoutService: ({ language, packageName }) =>
    problem(`The package ${packageName} exports no language service for ${language}.`),
  PackageNotLoaded: ({ packageName, cause }) =>
    problem(`The package ${packageName} could not be loaded.`, reasonOf(cause)),
  TropeRefused: ({ module, reason }) =>
    problem(`Loom refuses the installed trope in ${module}.`, reasonOf(reason)),
}

const isWordedBy =
  <Failure extends { readonly _tag: string }>(wording: Wording<Failure>) =>
  (value: unknown): value is Failure =>
    typeof value === 'object' &&
    value !== null &&
    '_tag' in value &&
    typeof value._tag === 'string' &&
    Object.keys(wording).includes(value._tag)

export const failureIn =
  <Failure extends { readonly _tag: string }>(wording: Wording<Failure>) =>
  (message: unknown): Option.Option<Failure> =>
    Option.filter(
      Option.fromNullishOr(Array.isArray(message) ? message[0] : message),
      isWordedBy(wording),
    )

## A mistake in the code of Loom

A defect is a mistake in Loom's own code, a failure no type foresaw: an exception from a library, or a value a schema should never have refused. Effect carries a defect in a cause, not in a type. Each program catches its defects where a piece of its work starts. Each defect becomes the failure `Broke`. So a mistake in the code reaches a person in the catalog's words, as every other failure does.

The failure `Broke` names the `work` the defect struck, the `subject` of that work, and the `cause`. The type `Work` lists the pieces of work a program catches defects around. The command line has one, a command. The language server has nine, such as opening a workspace, rebuilding a loom after an edit, or listing the books of a workspace. The subject is the loom or the workspace the work was on, and is empty for work that has none. The table `works` gives each piece of work its summary.

The function `broke` makes one from the cause Effect hands over. A cause may hold a failure, a defect, or several, and Effect's function `Cause.squash` picks the one at its heart.

=>

export type Work =
  | 'command'
  | 'opening'
  | 'warming'
  | 'projecting'
  | 'forgetting'
  | 'reprojecting'
  | 'rereading'
  | 'rebuilding'
  | 'keeping'
  | 'listing'

export class Broke extends Data.TaggedError('Broke')<{
  readonly work: Work
  readonly subject: string
  readonly cause: string
}> {}

export const broke = (work: Work, subject: string, cause: Cause.Cause<unknown>): Broke =>
  new Broke({ work, subject, cause: reasonOf(Cause.squash(cause)) })

const works: { readonly [Each in Work]: (subject: string) => string } = {
  command: () => "Something broke in Loom's command line.",
  opening: (workspace) => `Something broke while Loom opened the workspace ${workspace}.`,
  warming: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom built the corpus of ${loom} ahead.`,
  projecting: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom prepared ${loom} for the editor.`,
  forgetting: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom forgot ${loom}.`,
  reprojecting: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom prepared ${loom} for the editor again.`,
  rereading: () => 'Something broke while Loom read the looms that changed on disk.',
  rebuilding: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom rebuilt ${loom} after an edit.`,
  keeping: (loom) => `Something broke while Loom kept the boxes of ${loom} in step.`,
  listing: (workspace) => `Something broke while Loom listed the books of ${workspace}.`,
}

const brokeWording: Wording<Broke> = {
  Broke: ({ work, subject, cause }) => problem(works[work](subject), reasonOf(cause)),
}

## One catalog

The type `LoomFailure` is the union of every failure above, and the table `failureWording` joins their wordings. The function `describe` reads a failure through that table. It is a matcher built once from the table, and it does not compile while a failure has no case.

A person reads the two parts of a problem as one line. The function `lineOf` joins them.

=>

export type LoomFailure =
  | ReadingFailure
  | WritingFailure
  | TanglingFailure
  | PackageFailure
  | KeepingFailure
  | AskingFailure
  | Warning
  | Broke

export const failureWording: Wording<LoomFailure> = {
  ...readingWording,
  ...writingWording,
  ...tanglingWording,
  ...packageWording,
  ...keepingWording,
  ...askingWording,
  ...warningWording,
  ...brokeWording,
}

export const describe: (failure: LoomFailure) => Problem = Match.type<LoomFailure>().pipe(
  Match.tagsExhaustive(failureWording),
)

export const lineOf = ({ summary, detail }: Problem): string =>
  detail === '' ? summary : `${summary} ${detail}`

## Where each shows

A failure shows where the person who can act on it is looking.

The command line has one place, standard error. It prints the problem's summary after its own name, `loom:`, and the detail on the line below. Under a tangle that was refused it lists the diagnostics that refused it. Then it ends with exit code 1. A warning is printed the same way where it is met, and the command goes on. The chapter "The Loom CLI" builds this in its section "Telling a failure".

The language server has four places.

- **The answer to a request.** A workspace that cannot be opened fails the editor's `initialize` request, and the answer carries the failure's line. So the editor hears of a wrong storage key as it connects.
- **The loom.** A loom that could not be read shows the failure as its one diagnostic. So does a loom read with delimiters Loom cannot use.
- **A language's package.** A package that Loom loads from the store cannot see this catalog. So a failure that crosses to one crosses as its line, in words. The TypeScript service is handed the reason a TypeScript is missing or was not fetched, and shows it in the looms it checks. Such a package words its own errors as well. The errors of the TypeScript service travel as rejected promises, which other code reads by their `message`, so each of them writes its own.
- **The log.** The server writes the failure `Broke` to its log, which an editor keeps in a panel of its own, with the whole cause. The server goes on after it, and the author can do nothing about it but report it. A warning lands in the log too, as its line.

## What the command line adds

The command line meets failures no other program does: a release it cannot reach when it upgrades itself, an editor's settings it will not overwrite, an argument a command cannot take. Their errors live in the command line's own package. That package builds on this one, so this catalog cannot name them.

The command line widens the catalog instead, in the chapter "The Loom CLI". Its own union adds its errors to the type `LoomFailure`. Its own wording spreads the table `failureWording` and adds a case for each of its errors. Its own function `describe` is built the same way from that wording, so the compiler holds the command line to both sets at once.

## What the catalog draws on

The catalog takes each error as a type alone, so it loads no module only to name that module's errors. It runs on Effect, and it reads the default pair of anchor delimiters for its advice. The errors of an application's requests come from the outline, the edits, and the server, and the error of a variable's name from the configuration. It sits beside the fault catalog, among the modules that read a loom, so the builder of one module takes the words of a loom it could not read from here.

=>

import { Cause, Data, Match, Option } from 'effect'
import { defaultAnchorDelims, type InvalidAnchorDelims } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import type {
  NotAVariableName,
  SettingsNotWritten,
  SettingsUnread,
} from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import type {
  PackageMissing,
  PackageNotLoaded,
  PackageStale,
  PackageWithoutService,
} from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageLoader'
import type { ServiceError } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService'
import type { StoreError } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import type { ReadError } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import type { TangleError } from '../LoomCompiler'
import type {
  BookExists,
  EditBreaksLoom,
  LoomExists,
  NoConfiguration,
  NoSuchBook,
  NoSuchNote,
  NoSuchPart,
  NoSuchSection,
  NotADiscussion,
  NotInPart,
  NotOfKind,
  OpensABook,
  OpensTheLoom,
  PartExists,
  PartNameIsNumber,
  SectionExists,
  TitleTaken,
  UnfitName,
  TakesNoParameters,
  UnfitParameter,
  UnfitText,
} from '../EditBuilder'
import type { NoSuchLoom } from '../OutlineBuilder'
